# Compilation

In HQL, **compilation** is an execution strategy, not a phase of the language.
What a program means is decided when it is parsed and checked; a backend then
executes the checked program, and compiling it is one way a backend may do
that. HQL has no compile time, and no rule of the language is stated against
one ([HQL-0044](../../proposals/HQL-0044/README.md)).

## The phases

```text
parse    source text        → syntax tree              writes: parse failure[unexpected-token], …
check    syntax tree        → checked program          writes: check error[unknown-name], check error[type-mismatch], …
lower    checked program    → execution tree
plan     execution tree     → stages per step          writes: plan failure[backend-refusal]
execute  planned tree       → value and diagnostics    writes: execute failure[division-by-zero], execute error[contract-violation], …
```

**Checking** is the semantic boundary. It resolves every name, import and
module, infers and checks every type, and verifies every trait implementation,
bound and coherence rule, over the whole program before any of it executes. A
program the checker rejects executes nothing, under `collect` as under
`strict`, and a document is checked as a whole before any cell runs. `hql
check` is that checking and nothing after it; `hql run`, `hql render` and
`hql repl` run the same checking first. The [core
architecture](../../models/domain/architecture.md) has the modules that
implement each phase.

**Execution** is a backend's ([[pipes]]). `localhost`, the only backend,
evaluates the execution tree one step after another, and it is the oracle: a
backend that compiles or JIT-compiles the tree must produce the value and
diagnostics `localhost` produces, accept exactly the programs the checker accepts,
and reject none on a ground the checker owns. A compilation step inside such a
backend runs after checking, and its failure is the backend's, never a
diagnostic about the program. A failure that depends on the values, an
overflow or a missing file, is an `execute` diagnostic the run writes into its
diagnostic channel ([[error-handling]]).

## Traits are checked, not compiled

A [[type-system|trait]] is a capability contract over a type, and the checker
verifies it: an implementation against its trait, a bound wherever it is used,
and one implementation per trait and type. A trait has no runtime
representation, so nothing about it changes between a backend that evaluates
and one that compiles; a call to a trait function runs the implementation of
the value's own type.

## Saying which phase acts

A sentence about a rejection names what rejects it or the diagnostic kind it
gives: "the checker rejects", "is a type error", "is a name error", "the
parser rejects". "Is refused" alone names neither. "Statically typed" stays the
tagline, since every value's type is known before the program runs; "compile
time" and "compile-time" describe no HQL rule. "Compiled into the binary"
speaks of the Rust implementation, where an extension is, and is accurate there
([[hql/extensions]]).
